{"sample_id": "chat-c262fed8-714a-4f8e-806d-db5fd13301a2", "timestamp": "2026-04-17T03:10:34.604663+00:00", "question": "I think my demo is just a simple application using ai agent, but I want to implement RAG system so I need to introduce memory management to generate more accurate response\n\n[System Note: The current workspace contains these files: MemoryBank.pdf, Memory-R1.pdf, SimpleMem.pdf. Please rely on your retrieval tools to read their contents.]", "contexts": ["part of the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework. The Memory Manager decides whether to ADD, UPDATE, DELETE, or NOOP, thereby maintaining and evolving the memory state. For question answering, the Answer Agent applies a memory distillation policy over retrieved memo- ries to filter noise and reason over the most relevant content. Both agents are fine-tuned with PPO or GRPO, enabling outcome-driven learning of mem- ory operations and selective utilization. Further implementation", "of a Memory Manager to perform structured memory operations and an Answer Agent to filter and reason over memories retrieved via RAG. (2) We develop a data-efficient fine-tuning strategy using PPO and GRPO that enables Memory-R1 to achieve strong performance with as few as 152 question–answer pairs, demonstrating that large memory improve- ments can be achieved with minimal supervision. (3) We provide in-depth analysis of RL choices, model size, and memory design, offering action- able insights", "partially constructed memory bank and (ii) a new dialogue turn with information relevant to downstream QA. The goal is to learn which op- eration produces a memory state that enables the 3 Figure 2: Overview of the Memory-R1 framework. Stage 1 (blue) constructs and updates the memory bank via the RL-fine-tuned Memory Manager, which chooses operations {ADD, UPDATE, DELETE, NOOP} for each new dialogue turn. Stage 2 (green) answers user questions via the Answer Agent, which applies a Memory", "advice Memory Storage Memory Updating Ebbinghaus Forgetting Curve Memory Strength Updating Relevant Memory User Portrait Event Summary Memory Retrieval Memory Augmented Prompt Figure 1: Overview of MemoryBank. The memory storage (§ 2.1) stores past conversations, summa- rized events and user portraits, while the memory updating mechanism (§ 2.3) updates the memory storage. Memory retrieval (§ 2.2) recall relevant memory. SiliconFriend (§ 3) serves as an LLM-based AI companion augmented with", "trade-off. Addi- tional analyses are provided in Appendix G. 5 Conclusion We presented Memory-R1, a reinforcement learn- ing framework that enables LLM-based agents to effectively manage and utilize external mem- ory. Unlike heuristic pipelines, Memory-R1 learns memory operations as well as memory distillation and usage for answering. With only 152 training examples, it achieves state-of-the-art results on Lo- CoMo, scales across model sizes, and generalizes to MSC and LongMemEval without"], "answer": "For your demo application using an AI agent with a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) system, **Memory-R1** may be the most suitable choice. It provides a structured memory management approach using reinforcement learning, allowing for efficient memory operations (ADD, UPDATE, DELETE) and effective retrieval through a Memory Manager. This can enhance response accuracy with minimal supervision, as it achieves strong performance with as few as 152 question-answer pairs. \n\nIn contrast, **MemoryBank** offers a modular architecture for memory storage and retrieval, but may require more complex implementation. Given your goal of simplicity and effectiveness, starting with Memory-R1 would likely yield quicker results in your demo.", "ground_truth": "", "metadata": {"source": "chat_endpoint", "stack_id": "45b9f1458788", "user_id": "workspace-user", "session_id": "workspace-session", "mode": "patterns", "model": "gpt-4o-mini"}}
